# AuthOnce security headers — Cloudflare Pages `_headers` convention.
# Added 2026-08-29 (T24-D). Read this whole file before touching it —
# several values here are specific fixes for a wallet-connecting dApp,
# not generic hardening, and the wrong value silently breaks wallet
# popups with no error message to the user.
#
# CSP is currently Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only, NOT enforced.
# Report-Only sends the exact same policy to the browser but never blocks
# anything — violations only show as warnings in the browser DevTools
# Console (Cloudflare Pages has no report-collector configured here, so
# there is no server-side report; check the console directly).
#
# Before flipping Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only to a real, enforced
# Content-Security-Policy header below: open the live site in a browser
# with DevTools Console open, and walk through every flow that talks to
# an external origin, watching for red "Refused to..." CSP messages:
#   1. Connect a wallet (MetaMask + WalletConnect QR, if you have a second
#      device to test with) on the homepage and on /pay/:merchant/:slug.
#   2. Subscribe to a test product (full permit-signing flow).
#   3. View /my-subscriptions (wallet-connect + sign-message flow).
#   4. Cancel a subscription (on-chain tx flow).
#   5. Load /admin and /pricing.
#   6. Click "Accept" on the cookie banner and confirm GA4 actually loads
#      (Network tab: a request to googletagmanager.com should appear).
# If the console is clean across all of that, it's safe to rename
# Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only to Content-Security-Policy below
# (delete the -Report-Only suffix) and redeploy. If anything is blocked,
# tell me exactly what the console message says — it names the exact
# blocked origin and directive, which is normally all that's needed to
# add one line to the relevant directive below.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://www.googletagmanager.com 'sha256-ON0Xd1FLnbiEXZD3CXl6NkXyXq2gRglSFhJcIiF8cGM=' 'sha256-A6faL9pEGrGIJJtExNuFwOILzu401VZNw0qZPwO2aIA=' 'sha256-N0zVAtHZTUjQ1kXyq/qo48QgoxGHmt0EiWJYPSVScxc=' 'sha256-Ml/qolLkRBuvZr/j6Gga2iOkJJxrCDBENTXU+PvLqzs=' 'sha256-T7kPZ7VH6pRhS4kD4BBb7gW0Q5G1b6f3zvR4ocA2uY4=' 'sha256-qByWsKBDz6EtSluvymY+iOhl6JDZnEUZbQIdpECuE68='; style-src 'self' https://fonts.googleapis.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com https://fonts.reown.com; connect-src 'self' https://the-opportunity-production.up.railway.app https://base-sepolia.g.alchemy.com https://sepolia.base.org https://84532.rpc.thirdweb.com https://rpc.walletconnect.com https://rpc.walletconnect.org https://relay.walletconnect.com https://relay.walletconnect.org wss://relay.walletconnect.com wss://relay.walletconnect.org https://pulse.walletconnect.com https://pulse.walletconnect.org https://api.web3modal.com https://api.web3modal.org https://keys.walletconnect.com https://keys.walletconnect.org https://notify.walletconnect.com https://notify.walletconnect.org https://echo.walletconnect.com https://echo.walletconnect.org https://push.walletconnect.com https://push.walletconnect.org wss://www.walletlink.org https://cca-lite.coinbase.com https://www.google-analytics.com https://analytics.google.com https://region1.google-analytics.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; frame-src 'self' https://verify.walletconnect.com https://verify.walletconnect.org https://secure.walletconnect.com https://secure.walletconnect.org; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# ─── Notes on each directive, and why it's shaped this way ──────────────
#
# script-src: 'self' + 6 sha256 hashes total. The first 3
# (ON0Xd1FL.../A6faL9pE.../N0zVAtHZ...) = the site's own 3 static JSON-LD
# <script> blocks in index.html (SoftwareApplication, FAQPage,
# Organization schema — SEO structured data, unchanged since T24). If any
# of those 3 blocks is ever edited, its hash MUST be recomputed and
# swapped in here, or that specific block silently stops being read as
# valid JSON-LD by anything that respects CSP (Google's renderer doesn't
# care, since script-src violations don't remove the DOM node, only some
# stricter crawlers might).
#
# The other 3 (Ml/qolLk.../T7kPZ7VH.../qByWsKBD...) were added 2026-08-30,
# same reason and same fragility: JSON-LD <script> blocks in
# docs/docs-ai-agents.html, docs/docs-saas.html, and docs/docs-web3.html
# respectively (one hash each, since the JSON-LD content differs per
# page). These 3 static pages are NOT part of the React SPA but ARE
# covered by this same site-wide `/*` header rule — they were found
# uncovered during the pre-enforcement audit (T24-D) and would have
# silently broken the moment Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only became
# enforced. At the same time, the OTHER inline scripts those 3 pages
# (plus legal.html) had — a shared gtag-init block and a shared
# scroll-spy block — were externalized instead of hashed, following the
# consent-banner.js precedent below: see docs/docs-analytics.js,
# docs/docs-scrollspy.js, and legal-tabs.js. Only the page-specific
# JSON-LD blocks still use the hash approach, because externalizing SEO
# structured data would defeat the point (it needs to stay inline for
# crawlers that read the page source directly).
#
# The cookie-consent banner is deliberately an EXTERNAL file
# (/consent-banner.js) specifically so it never needs a hash — 'self'
# covers it automatically, immune to future edits. googletagmanager.com
# is here because consent-banner.js dynamically injects a
# <script src="...googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?...">> only after
# consent, AND because docs-analytics.js (the 3 docs pages' externalized
# gtag-init file) loads unconditionally on page load, no consent gate —
# CSP still checks a dynamically-created script's origin against
# script-src either way.
#
# IMPORTANT for whoever adds the next static HTML page under
# frontend/public/: this file's script-src does NOT auto-cover new
# inline <script> blocks. Follow the same pattern — put any reusable
# logic in its own .js file under /self (like docs-analytics.js /
# docs-scrollspy.js / legal-tabs.js), and hash-pin only page-specific
# inline content that must stay inline (e.g. JSON-LD). A new inline
# <script> block that nobody externalizes or hashes will silently fail
# once CSP is enforced (or show up as a Report-Only console warning
# before that) — this is a manual convention, not something the build
# enforces automatically.
#
# style-src: 'self' + Google Fonts stylesheet only — NO 'unsafe-inline'.
# Confirmed safe: React's style={{}} prop (used everywhere in this app)
# sets individual style properties via the DOM style API, which CSP does
# NOT restrict — only literal style="..." HTML attributes and cssText
# bulk-assignment are restricted, and this codebase has neither (no
# styled-components/Emotion, and consent-banner.css was written as real
# CSS classes for exactly this reason). If a future change adds a
# style="..." literal attribute or a CSS-in-JS library, this breaks and
# needs 'unsafe-inline' added back.
#
# img-src: uses the https: scheme wildcard rather than an exact domain
# list. Wallet/token icon CDNs vary unpredictably across chains and
# registries (WalletConnect's own official CSP guidance does the same —
# see Reown's docs). img-src leaking to an unexpected host is a much
# lower-severity issue (referrer/tracking-pixel style) than script-src or
# connect-src leaking, so this is a deliberate, asymmetric trade-off, not
# an oversight.
#
# connect-src: 'self' + Railway (API) + Alchemy/Base/Thirdweb (RPC,
# matches frontend/src/config.js's RPC_URLS exactly) + the full
# WalletConnect/Reown domain list (sourced from Reown's own official CSP
# documentation, docs.reown.com/advanced/security/content-security-policy,
# fetched 2026-08-29) + Google Analytics' actual data-collection endpoints
# (separate from the googletagmanager.com script-loader domain above).
# This is the highest-value directive in this whole policy — it's what
# stops an XSS payload from exfiltrating data or driving wallet calls to
# an attacker's server, so don't loosen it casually.
#
# frame-src / frame-ancestors: frame-src allows Reown's verify/secure
# iframes (used during wallet connection for domain verification).
# frame-ancestors 'none' means no other site can iframe authonce.io at
# all — confirmed safe 2026-08-29: no live embed feature exists (the
# "Embeddable widget" in Pricing.jsx/LandingPage.jsx marketing copy is
# roadmap-only, marked done:false in LandingPage.jsx's own roadmap data —
# worth flagging separately if that copy oversells something not built
# yet, same B7 pattern found before). If an embeddable widget ships later,
# frame-ancestors needs a per-path exception for that route specifically
# — do NOT loosen it site-wide for that.
#
# Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups, NOT the stricter
# same-origin. This exact value is Reown's own explicit recommendation —
# same-origin (no popups suffix) breaks the postMessage communication
# between this tab and a wallet-extension popup window. Do not "fix" this
# to same-origin without checking that guidance again.
#
# Trusted Types: deliberately NOT included. Enabling it requires auditing
# every innerHTML/dangerouslySetInnerHTML call site in the codebase and
# defining a policy for each — not attempted in this pass, flagged as a
# separate future item rather than risking a rushed, breaking rollout.
#
# X-Frame-Options: DENY is redundant with frame-ancestors 'none' in any
# browser that supports CSP (which is effectively all of them today), but
# costs nothing to include as defense-in-depth for the rare exception.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security is DELIBERATELY NOT set in this file — it's
# controlled from the Cloudflare dashboard instead (SSL/TLS → Edge
# Certificates → HSTS on the authonce.io zone), and confirmed 2026-08-29
# that the dashboard setting wins over whatever this file says: this file
# originally shipped `max-age=63072000` here, and it never took effect —
# `curl -D -` kept showing the old dashboard value regardless. Putting a
# value here that silently does nothing is worse than not having it, so
# it's been removed. Live history: was max-age=15552000 (180 days, below
# Chrome's HSTS preload-list floor of >= 31536000/1 year, so "preload" was
# already set but not actually valid) — the Cloudflare dashboard's own
# Max-Age dropdown caps out at 12 months (31536000), which is what it's
# set to now. That exactly meets the preload floor; 2 years isn't
# reachable through this control plane, only via the Cloudflare API
# directly. If HSTS ever needs to change again, do it in the dashboard,
# not here — this file's job for HSTS is just this comment, as a pointer
# for whoever looks here first.
#
# X-Content-Type-Options is ALSO independently set by a Cloudflare
# dashboard toggle on the same HSTS settings panel ("No-Sniff Header"),
# already on, same value (nosniff) — unlike HSTS this one isn't a known
# conflict since both sides agree, so the line here is kept as the
# authoritative, explicit statement of intent; just don't be surprised if
# turning it off here alone doesn't fully remove the header.
